{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790732758,"nonce":"POpQZfy30ycuAGP2xyK8IMe72w8=","title":"BytesData views and imported fastGet break TestBytes","message":"The tiered conformance case TestBytes did not compile because rayzor.Bytes lacked ofData, fastGet and getString. Once those methods were exposed, its blit tests revealed the native byte API used source-first parameters while Haxe Bytes.blit is destination-first. Its readAll path returned empty bytes through the inherited Input method, and an imported fastGet alias crashed because the call was lowered as a function value rather than a static runtime call.\n\nBytesData is represented as Array\u003cInt\u003e with eight-byte slots while native Bytes stores one-byte elements. getData therefore needs a view registry and slot synchronization so writes through Bytes and writes through the array remain visible to each other. The regression BytesDataViews checks direct indexing, imported fastGet, array writes and ofData aliasing.\n\nBefore: TestBytes COMPILE_FAIL, 781/1094 scored passes in /tmp/rayzor-bytes-restricted-full/report.tsv. After: TestBytes PASS with 196 assertions, 783/1094 in /tmp/rayzor-bytesdata-final-full/report.tsv. Issue10083 also changed WRONG_ANSWER to PASS but is known to fluctuate between runs, so TestBytes is the stable improvement. All 94 authored regressions pass.\n\nThe required cargo clippy --workspace --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings run stops on existing unused-unsafe warnings in runtime/src/profile.rs and needless-bool in runtime/src/type_system.rs. A separate root release build also encounters unrelated GPU host ABI compilation errors. Those are outside this Bytes change.","files":null}]}